<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  org.nextbsd.getty.plist

  The one login getty. It picks exactly one terminal per boot:

    1. The loader was asked for a serial console
       (console contains comconsole, or boot_serial=YES)
         -> getty on /dev/console, which the kernel bound to the UART.
            This is CI, and any serial-console server.
    2. Otherwise, if there is a screen (/dev/ttyv0 exists)
         -> getty on ttyv0.
    3. Otherwise (headless, no framebuffer)
         -> getty on /dev/console.

  Why it replaces the two jobs that were here (org.nextbsd.getty.console
  and org.nextbsd.getty.ttyv0):
    /dev/console binds to exactly ONE tty: cnadd() points it at the first
    console and cnselect() moves the chosen one to the head of cn_devlist
    (sys/kern/kern_cons.c). Which tty that is depends on the machine:

      arm64 with a screen: the EFI loader publishes hw.uart.console from
        ACPI SPCR without being asked, so /dev/console is the UART and the
        screen got no login unless a second job served ttyv0.
      amd64 laptop: /dev/console IS ttyv0, so both jobs targeted the same
        tty. The second one's login_tty() failed with EPERM (the tty
        already belonged to the first job's session, sys/kern/tty.c
        TIOCSCTTY), getty logged "login_tty /dev/ttyv0: Operation not
        permitted", exited, and KeepAlive respawned it every 10 seconds,
        forever.

    Deciding from the loader's own settings covers both with one job: a
    serial console is only preferred when someone asked for it, and
    otherwise the login goes where a person is looking. Measured on an
    amd64 ThinkPad T460s and an arm64 virt VM: one job, getty on v0, no
    log spam; with boot_serial=YES set in kenv the job moves to the UART.

  Why ttyv0 is there early enough on arm64:
    vt(4) attaches with the kernel's own virtio_gpu / efifb driver before
    launchd starts; the DRM kext loaded later only replaces the driver
    ("VT: Replacing driver ... with new drmfb"), not the terminal.

  A serial login on a machine that also has a screen:
    set boot_serial="YES" (or console="comconsole") in /boot/loader.conf.

  Why 3wire.9600 (nextbsd#355):
    FreeBSD's std.* gettytab classes lack nc (no carrier), so on a UART
    without DCD the post-login shell hangs opening /dev/tty. 3wire.9600
    sets CLOCAL. On vt(4) carrier always asserts, so it is harmless there.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.nextbsd.getty</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/sh</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>case "$(kenv -q console)" in *comconsole*) exec /usr/libexec/getty 3wire.9600 console ;; esac; case "$(kenv -q boot_serial)" in [Yy][Ee][Ss]) exec /usr/libexec/getty 3wire.9600 console ;; esac; test -c /dev/ttyv0 &amp;&amp; exec /usr/libexec/getty 3wire.9600 ttyv0; exec /usr/libexec/getty 3wire.9600 console</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
